<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="fatty acid biosynthesis initiation (plant mitochondria)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6799" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; In plants, the major site for fatty acid biosynthesis is plastids. However it is known that mitochondria of eukaryotes are also capable of de novo fatty acid biosynthesis. For example, synthesis of fatty acyl-ACP from labeled malonate was detected in isolated mitochondria from pea |CITS: [10671542]|, and from Neurospora |CITS: [2137086]|. The plant mitochondrial pathway differs from the |FRAME: PWY-4381 &quot;plastidic pathway&quot;|. First, in the mitochondrial pathway, malonate, not acetyl-CoA, is the sole precursor of fatty acids, although the source of malonate is still poorly understood |CITS: [10671542]|. Second, malonyl-ACP serves as both the primer and donor substrates for de novo fatty acid synthesis |CITS: [14660674]|. Third, there is only one condensing enzyme involved in the mitochondrial pathway. In contrast to the three condensing enzymes in the plastids, KASIII, KASI and KASII, where each carry out the initial condensation, elongation from C4 to C16, and the elongation from C16 to C18 reactions, respectively, the mitochondrial condensing enzyme carries out both the priming and all the elongating reactions |CITS: [14660674]|. The biological role of the mitochondrial pathway is not entirely clear. One role is to provide octanoyl-ACP for the |FRAME: PWY0-501 &quot;biosynthesis of lipoate&quot;|, an important sulfur-containing cofactor of a number of enzymes such as glycine decarboxylase complex and pyruvate dehydrogenase |CITS: [10671542]|. Another role is likely to provide fatty acyl chains for the |FRAME: PWY-5269 &quot;synthesis of cardiolipin&quot;|, a lipid unique to mitochondrial membranes |CITS: [Li-Beisson2010]|. The pathway does not appear to operate in grasses (Poaceae) |CITS: [12972648]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
